A binary operation $O:[0,1]^2\to[0,1]$ is called power-stable if $O(x^p,y^p)=O(x,y)^p$ for every $p>0$ and every $x,y\in[0,1]$. It is known that any such operation admits the two representations
$$O(x,y)=(xy)^{f\left(\tfrac{\log x}{\log(xy)}\right)} \qquad\text{and}\qquad O(x,y)=(xy)^{g\left(\tfrac{\log x}{\log y}\right)},$$
where $f$ and $g$ are one-variable functions (with $f$ defined on $[0,1]$ and $g$ defined on $[0,\infty]$, suitable conventions handling boundary cases $x=1$, $y=1$, $x=y=1$, etc.).

Establish precise correspondences between properties of the binary operation $O$ and properties of the generating functions $f$ and $g$. In particular, characterize, in terms of $f$ (and separately in terms of $g$), exactly when $O$ has each of the following properties:
(i) monotonicity (non-decreasing in each variable);
(ii) continuity on $[0,1]^2$;
(iii) the 1-Lipschitz property with respect to a chosen norm on $[0,1]^2$;
(iv) associativity, i.e.\ $O(O(x,y),z)=O(x,O(y,z))$ for all $x,y,z\in[0,1]$;
(v) existence of a neutral element $e\in[0,1]$, i.e.\ $O(e,x)=O(x,e)=x$ for all $x\in[0,1]$.

